import Doc from '@/layouts/Doc.astro'
import Command from '@/components/Command.astro'
import Waterfall from '@/components/Waterfall.astro'
---

<Doc
  title="Regression testing"
  description="Turn a production run that went wrong into a permanent test case, run mcpd's built-in scorers over stored runs, and compare two versions of an agent with the sample sizes reported beside the deltas."
  lede="The run that broke on Tuesday becomes the case every later version has to pass."
  og="docs"
>
  <h2>The loop this exists to close</h2>
  <p>
    An agent fails on a real question. Somebody reproduces it by hand, changes a
    prompt, and it looks better. Three weeks later it fails on the same question
    again, because nothing kept it.
  </p>
  <p>
    A dataset keeps it. Capture the failing run, and it stops being an anecdote
    and becomes a case with an input, an expected answer when you have one, and
    a pointer back to the trace it came from. Every later version runs against
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>Capturing a run</h2>
  <p>
    Find the run in the interface or through the API, then hand its trace id to
    the dataset.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="capture a failed run as a case"
    command={`curl -X POST localhost:4317/v1/api/datasets/$DATASET/capture \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"trace_ids": ["b3d9e71a4c0f428eb6a5d2317f90c684"]}'`}
  />

  <p>
    The captured case keeps <code>source_trace_id</code>, so months later you can
    open the run that motivated it and read what actually happened rather than
    guessing from the input.
  </p>

  <Waterfall class="not-prose my-8" />

  <h2>The run's own answer is not the expected answer</h2>
  <p>
    <code>use_output_as_expected</code> is off by default, and that default is
    the important part. The runs worth capturing are usually the ones that went
    wrong, and recording a wrong answer as the reference would enshrine the bug:
    every later version would then be measured on its ability to reproduce it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turn it on when you are capturing runs that were right, to hold a behaviour
    you already like in place. Leave it off, and write the expected answer
    yourself, when you are capturing a failure.
  </p>

  <h2>The built-in scorers</h2>
  <p>
    mcpd ships thirteen deterministic scorers: ordinary code that reads a stored
    run and returns a number. They cost nothing, they never call a model, and
    they give the same answer twice, which is the property a regression test
    needs and a judge model does not have.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scorer</th><th>Passes when</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>answered</code></td><td>The run produced a non-empty answer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>no_errors</code></td><td>No span in the run failed.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>exact_match</code></td><td>The answer equals the expected one.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>contains</code></td><td>The answer contains a required string.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>json_valid</code></td><td>The answer parses as JSON.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>latency_budget</code></td><td>The run finished inside a millisecond budget.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cost_budget</code></td><td>The run cost no more than a set amount.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>no_tool_loop</code></td><td>No tool was called twice with the same input.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tool_efficiency</code></td><td>The run stayed inside a tool-call budget.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>not_a_refusal</code></td><td>The answer is not a refusal or an apology.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>no_leaked_secret</code></td><td>No key, token or credential appears in the answer.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>span_present</code></td><td>The run recorded a span of the required kind or name.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>tool_succeeded</code></td><td>The required tool ran, and at least one call of it ended ok.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    <code>GET /v1/api/scorers</code> returns the same list with a
    <code>needs_config</code> flag. A scorer that needs an expected answer and is
    not given one declines rather than guessing, and the decline is reported.
  </p>

  <h2>When a model does the grading</h2>
  <p>
    Some questions no amount of deterministic code answers. Whether an answer
    actually addressed the question, whether a summary kept the meaning, whether
    a tone was right: those need a reader, and a model is the cheap reader.
  </p>
  <p>
    Judged scoring is off until you configure it, and a judged scorer never runs
    unless you name it. Both defaults exist for the same reason: grading spends
    money on every run it reads, and a feature that quietly does that is a
    feature you find out about on an invoice.
  </p>

  <pre><code class="language-json">{`{
  "judge": {
    "endpoint": "http://localhost:11434/v1",
    "model": "qwen2.5:14b",
    "api_key": "",
    "timeout_seconds": 30,
    "criteria": {
      "answer_relevance": "Does the answer address the question that was asked?",
      "no_hedging": "Does the answer commit, rather than hedge?"
    }
  }
}`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The request is the OpenAI chat-completions shape, which is not a preference
    for a vendor: Ollama, vLLM, llama.cpp, LM Studio, OpenRouter and the hosted
    providers all accept it, so the same configuration reaches a model on your
    laptop and a gateway in production. Leave <code>api_key</code> empty for a
    local model that wants none. The temperature is zero, because a judge that
    answers differently each time cannot be compared with itself, which is the
    only thing scores are for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Criteria are configured rather than passed on each request. A scorer id is
    stored on every score it produces and is what a later comparison groups by,
    so letting each call invent both the id and the question would build a
    column nobody can trust.
  </p>
  <p>
    Configured criteria do not appear in <code>GET /v1/api/scorers</code>. That
    endpoint lists the thirteen built-in scorers and nothing else, so a client
    that builds its scorer menu from it will not offer your criteria. The one
    place the daemon names them back to you is the error you get for an unknown
    scorer id, which lists the built-ins and the configured criteria together.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="run a judged scorer over the newest run"
    command={`curl -X POST localhost:4317/v1/api/score \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"limit": 1, "scorers": ["answer_relevance"]}'`}
  />

  <p>
    <code>timeout_seconds</code> is how long one grading call may take, from
    connecting to the last byte of the reply: 30 by default, from 1 to 300. A
    call that runs past it is declined with the key named, and mcpd reads at
    most 1 MiB of a reply.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each judged scorer is one model call per run, and the request has to finish
    inside <code>--request-timeout-seconds</code>. So one request grades at most
    as many runs as fit in that deadline if every call takes the whole of
    <code>timeout_seconds</code>. With the defaults, 30 and 30, and one judged
    scorer, that is one run. A request for more, in <code>trace_ids</code> or
    <code>limit</code>, is refused with a 400 that names the cap, before any
    model is called. Leave <code>limit</code> out and the request grades as many of
    the newest runs as fit. Raise <code>--request-timeout-seconds</code> to grade more in one
    request. Each judged score is stored as soon as the model answers, so a
    request cut short keeps the scores it finished.
  </p>

  <h3>It declines rather than guesses</h3>
  <p>
    A judge that returns a number it did not derive is worse than no judge,
    because it looks like evidence. So every one of these is reported in
    <code>skipped</code> with its reason, and none of them produces a score.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>What happened</th><th>What mcpd does</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>The run recorded no answer</td>
        <td>Declines. A run with no answer has not scored badly on relevance, it has not been measured.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The model replied in prose</td>
        <td>Declines, quoting the reply. JSON wrapped in a fenced block or a sentence is still read.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The model answered outside the scale</td>
        <td>Declines. A seven on a scale of one is not a nine tenths, and clamping it would invent a number.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The model could not be reached</td>
        <td>Declines, with the status and the body. A rate limit is a reason, not a zero.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The reply was larger than 1 MiB</td>
        <td>Declines without reading the rest. A verdict is a few hundred bytes, so a reply that size is not one.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>The request deadline left no time for the call</td>
        <td>Declines without calling the model. The pass answers with the scores it finished, inside the deadline, rather than a 503.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Every judged score records the model as its <code>scorer_version</code>.
    Two scores from two different judges are not comparable, and that field is
    what says so on the record.
  </p>

  <h2>Scoring happens after the fact</h2>
  <p>
    <code>POST /v1/api/score</code> runs scorers over runs that are already
    stored. Nothing evaluates inline. An evaluation in the request path adds its
    latency to the thing it is judging, and an evaluation that throws should
    never fail a user's request.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="score the last hundred runs"
    command={`curl -X POST localhost:4317/v1/api/score \\
  -H 'content-type: application/json' \\
  -d '{"project": "support-desk", "limit": 100, "config": {"max_ms": 4000}}'`}
  />

  <p>
    The response carries the scores it wrote and, separately, every scorer that
    declined with the reason it declined. A scorer that could not answer is more
    interesting than one that returned a number, so mcpd never swallows it. The
    project's ingest ceiling reaches this door too: a score the pass computed
    that would take the project over <code>--quota-per-minute</code> is not
    stored, and it is listed in <code>skipped</code> saying so, rather than
    written past the ceiling or thrown away in silence.
  </p>
  <p>
    Send <code>"dry_run": true</code> to see what a pass would produce without
    storing anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which runs a pass reads is decided by <code>trace_ids</code>,
    <code>from</code> and <code>limit</code>. Name the runs in
    <code>trace_ids</code> to score exactly those. Leave it empty and the pass
    takes the newest <code>limit</code> runs in the project, newest first, and
    <code>from</code> bounds that page: a pass sent <code>from</code> reads only
    runs that started at or after it, which is how you score everything since a
    deploy without listing ids.
  </p>

  <h2>Comparing two versions</h2>
  <p>
    Record an experiment for each version you want to compare, tag its scores
    with that experiment's id, and ask mcpd for the difference.
  </p>

  <Command
    class="not-prose my-6"
    title="did the new prompt help"
    command="curl 'localhost:4317/v1/api/experiments/compare?baseline=v3&candidate=v4'"
  />

  <p>
    The answer is a per-scorer delta with the sample size on each side, and a
    <code>decidable</code> flag. Below twenty observations mcpd says the
    comparison cannot be decided rather than reporting a movement.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is a deliberately blunt rule and not a significance test. The failure it
    prevents is the common one: a mean that moved on four runs, printed without
    its count, read as a result and shipped. Printing the counts beside the
    delta is what stops noise being mistaken for a finding.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this runs</h2>
  <p>
    All of it is the HTTP API, so the loop belongs in CI: capture failures as
    they happen, score the runs your test suite produces, register the build as
    an experiment, and fail the job when <code>regressed</code> comes back true
    on a decidable comparison.
  </p>

